---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { apps } from '../../data/site';
const app = apps.find((item) => item.slug === 'killmyapps')!;
---
<BaseLayout title="KillMyApps" description={app.description}>
  <div class="container page">
    <p><a class="back-link" href="/projects/">← Projects</a></p>
    <header class="page-header project-detail-header"><span class="eyebrow">Android Utility</span><h1>{app.name}</h1><p>{app.tagline}</p><div class="actions"><a class="button primary" href={app.download}>Download</a><a class="button" href={app.github}>View on GitHub</a></div></header>
    <figure class="project-banner"><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/KillMyAppsBanner.png" alt="KillMyApps banner" loading="eager" /></figure>
    <section class="detail-intro"><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.version}</strong><span>Version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.minAndroid}</strong><span>Minimum version</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>Root / Shizuku</strong><span>Execution layers</span></div><div class="detail-stat"><strong>{app.license}</strong><span>Open source</span></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Overview</span><h2>Focused process control.</h2><p>{app.description}</p><p>The main workflow is deliberately short: choose an application and terminate it. App discovery, automation, widgets, and Quick Settings extend that workflow without turning the project into a general-purpose app manager.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Why it exists</span><h2>A shorter path to a system action.</h2><p>Some applications continue running or restart in the background, while reaching the relevant controls through Android settings can take several steps. KillMyApps provides a dedicated interface for users who need to terminate selected processes repeatedly.</p><p>Root and Shizuku handle the privileged part of the operation while the application manages selection and interaction.</p></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Implementation</span><h2>Features around the same core action.</h2><div class="grid"><article class="card"><h3>Root & Shizuku</h3><p>Two privileged execution paths for the underlying process operation.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>App sources</h3><p>User, Launchable, and System sources provide different ways to find installed applications.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Selection Mode</h3><p>Choose the applications to terminate instead of working from a fixed list.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Automation</h3><p>Auto Killer can repeat the operation for selected background processes.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Quick access</h3><p>A Quick Settings Tile and home-screen widgets expose the main action outside the app.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Shared workflow</h3><p>Selection, filtering, automation, and shortcuts use the same underlying operation.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Architecture</span><h2>Separate selection from execution.</h2><p>The UI manages application discovery, filtering, and selection while the privileged execution layer handles the system operation. Root and Shizuku stay behind that boundary, allowing the rest of the workflow to remain independent of the selected access method.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">System integration</span><h2>Working outside the application sandbox.</h2><p>Terminating another application's process requires access beyond the normal Android application sandbox. The project uses Root or Shizuku for that privileged operation and keeps the rest of the interaction within the application layer.</p></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Performance</span><h2>Keep the common path small.</h2><p>Filtering, selection, background work, and privileged execution are kept separate so the main interaction does not carry unnecessary work. Less frequently used controls remain secondary to the primary termination workflow.</p></section>
    <section class="section screenshots-section"><span class="eyebrow">Screenshots</span><h2>The interface in use.</h2><div class="screenshot-grid"><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot2.png" alt="KillMyApps dark theme" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot3.png" alt="KillMyApps Quick Settings tile" loading="lazy" /></figure><figure><img src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dedeadend/KillMyApps/master/screenshots/screenshot1.png" alt="KillMyApps light theme" loading="lazy" /></figure></div></section>
    <section class="section"><span class="eyebrow">Release history</span><h2>Version history.</h2><div class="release-list"><div><strong>v1.5</strong><span>Auto Killer, 1×1 and 1×3 widgets, Shizuku UserService/AIDL work, Android 17 support, UI and performance changes.</span></div><div><strong>v1.4</strong><span>Quick Settings Tile, Selection Mode, animation controls, Shizuku integration, filtering and performance changes.</span></div><div><strong>v1.3</strong><span>Shizuku support and Killer Mode.</span></div><div><strong>v1.2</strong><span>Further changes to the core utility and workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.1</strong><span>Post-stable fixes and changes to the core workflow.</span></div><div><strong>v1.0.0</strong><span>First stable release.</span></div></div></section>
    <section class="section prose"><span class="eyebrow">Design decision</span><h2>Keep the scope narrow.</h2><p>The project adds features only when they support the same core operation across the app, widgets, Quick Settings, and automation. It is not intended to replace a full application manager.</p></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
